<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Venlo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1543">1543</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelders-wars">Guelders Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venlo">Venlo</a> was concluded on 7 September 1543 between Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and Duke William of Jülich-Cleves-Berg. After a swift campaign by Charles, William renounced the Duchy of Guelders and the County of Zutphen and promised to keep his lands Catholic. Guelders became one of the Seventeen Provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-venlo.jpg" alt="Treaty of Venlo" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Duchy_of_Guelders_and_County_of_Zutphen_(1350).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelders-wars">Guelders Wars</a>, which the treaty ended, grew out of an old dispute. At the end of the Second War of the Guelderian Succession (1423–1448), Gerhard VII, Duke of Jülich-Berg, sold his claims to the Duchy of Guelders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. Philip's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> exploited a quarrel in the ruling House of Egmont between Duke Arnold and his son Adolf to seize Guelders in 1473. After Charles died in 1477, his daughter Mary married Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and the Burgundian lands passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>.</p><p>The States of Guelders used the confusion to declare independence and proclaim Adolf's son Charles II as duke, and the Guelderian War of Independence (1477–1499) ended in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> defeat. From 1502, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Charles II fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-handsome">Philip the Handsome</a> and then his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> for 36 years, at times holding Oversticht, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nedersticht">Nedersticht</a>, the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ommelanden">Ommelanden</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>, and raiding Holland and Brabant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Charles II died without an heir in 1538, and the States of Guelders elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-duke-of-julich-cleves-berg">William, Duke of Jülich-Cleves-Berg</a>, to succeed him. By 1543 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> troops occupied all of Guelders, Zutphen and Jülich, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> failed to come to William's aid, so he sued for peace. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venlo">Venlo</a> he gave up his claims to Guelders and Zutphen in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a> and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and committed himself to suppressing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> in his remaining lands of Jülich, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, Berg, Mark and Ravenstein.</p><p>The treaty brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelders-wars">Guelders Wars</a> of 1502–1543 to a close and completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> acquisition of the Duchy of Guelders and the adjoining County of Zutphen, which were added to the Habsburg Netherlands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Venlo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke William of Cleves lost the duchy to Charles V and ceded it by the <b>Treaty of Venlo</b> in 1543, joining it to the Habsburg Netherlands.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Duchy of Guelders</a></li></ul></section>
